1.1.text:
Article 4
The scope of regulation for the protection of women and children from acts of violence includes:
a. implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. obligations and responsibilities;
c. forms of violence;
d. rights of women and children from acts of violence;
e. institutional matters;
f. cooperation and partnership;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. awards; and
i. funding;

Article 14
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a, include:
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs, and carrying out cooperation activities in the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutions for the protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating a budget for the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity;
d. fostering and supervising the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children who are victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for children who are victims of acts of violence.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes protection action programs and activities for women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the RPJMD.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 16
Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of violence against women and children is obligated to:
a. prevent and stop the ongoing act of violence;
b. provide protection to the victim;
c. provide emergency assistance; and/or
d. assist in the process of submitting an application for the determination of protection.

Article 6
The Regional Government in the implementation of Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence as referred to in Article 5 shall carry out:
a. prevention of acts of violence against women and children;
b. legal protection;
c. recovery;
d. coordination; and
e. encouraging community participation.

Article 7
- 7 - (1) The Regional Government shall prevent acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter a through empowerment and awareness-raising for Families, Parents, and the Community by providing information, guidance and/or outreach.
(2) In addition to the empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), implementation shall be carried out by means of:
a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in both formal and informal education;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern toward the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, educational institutions, and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening an integrated service system for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in every urban village (kelurahan).

Article 3
The objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence are to:
a. prevent acts of violence against women and children, including human trafficking;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children who are victims of violence, to reporters, and to witnesses; and
e. strengthen women and children who are victims of violence so that they are more empowered physically, psychologically, socially, and economically.
- 6 -

Article 8
(1) Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 7, shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population administration and civil registration;
f. women's empowerment, protection of women and children;
g. cooperatives and Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises; and
h. public tranquility, public order, and community protection.
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article 2
The protection of women and children from acts of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. humanity;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. public order and legal certainty;
e. transparency; and
f. protection.

Article 12
The Regional Government encourages community participation as referred to in Article 6 letter e, by means of:
a. fostering community awareness of cases of violence against women and children;
b. encouraging the community to actively participate in providing information and reporting acts of violence against women and children;
c. fostering local wisdom in handling cases of violence;
d. organizing strengthening of community groups in handling acts of violence against women and children;
e. disseminating information about laws and regulations related to acts of violence against women and children.

CHAPTER VIII
GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION
Article 25
(1) The Regional Government conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(2) Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. coordination;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.

CHAPTER IV
FORMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 17
Forms of violence against women and children include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. maltreatment;
f. exploitation; and/or
g. other violence.

Article 23
(1) The Regional Government develops cooperation patterns for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is in order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is conducted between the Regional Government and:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regional Governments;
d. Higher Education Institutions;
e. Social and Religious Institutions;
f. Non-Governmental Organizations; and/or
g. Media.
(4) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses.
(5) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER V
RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 18
Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights: a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected; b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable and in accordance with needs;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance; and
j. the right to a sense of security and protection.

