Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights: a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected; b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable and in accordance with needs;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance; and
j. the right to a sense of security and protection.

Article 7
- 7 - (1) The Regional Government shall prevent acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter a through empowerment and awareness-raising for Families, Parents, and the Community by providing information, guidance and/or outreach.
(2) In addition to the empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), implementation shall be carried out by means of:
a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in both formal and informal education;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern toward the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, educational institutions, and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening an integrated service system for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in every urban village (kelurahan).

Article 14
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a, include:
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs, and carrying out cooperation activities in the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutions for the protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating a budget for the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity;
d. fostering and supervising the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children who are victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for children who are victims of acts of violence.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes protection action programs and activities for women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the RPJMD.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 3
The objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence are to:
a. prevent acts of violence against women and children, including human trafficking;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children who are victims of violence, to reporters, and to witnesses; and
e. strengthen women and children who are victims of violence so that they are more empowered physically, psychologically, socially, and economically.
